        "model_answer": "The line graph illustrates global energy consumption derived from three renewable sources—hydroelectric, wind, and solar power—measured in gigawatt-hours (GWh) over a 24-year timeframe from 2000 to 2024.\n\nOverall, consumption across all three renewable sectors experienced substantial growth throughout the period. While hydroelectricity was by far the dominant renewable source during the first decade, both wind and solar power exhibited exponential growth in later years, ultimately surpassing hydroelectric generation by 2024.\n\nIn 2000, hydroelectricity started at approximately 2,500 GWh, steadily climbing to 3,400 GWh in 2010 before reaching 4,500 GWh by the end of the survey period. In contrast, wind and solar power recorded modest beginnings, generating just 300 GWh and 50 GWh respectively in 2000.\n\nHowever, from 2010 onwards, generation from wind and solar surged dramatically. Wind energy consumption rose sharply to 3,800 GWh in 2020, eventually peaking at 5,100 GWh in 2024 to become the leading renewable power source. Solar power demonstrated an even steeper upward trajectory, skyrocketing from 400 GWh in 2010 to 4,800 GWh in 2024, overtaking hydroelectricity in the final years.",
