        "model_answer": "In recent years, the allocation of national infrastructure budgets between public transit and road network expansion has sparked considerable debate. I firmly agree with the view that prioritizing investment in comprehensive public transportation systems offers vastly superior environmental, economic, and social advantages compared to constructing additional roadways.\n\nThe primary rationale for prioritizing public transit lies in environmental sustainability and urban decongestion. Private vehicular traffic is among the leading contributors to urban air pollution and greenhouse gas emissions. When state authorities invest in high-frequency metro lines, electric bus networks, and commuter trains, they provide viable, cost-effective alternatives that persuade commuters to leave their cars at home. Conversely, urban planning research demonstrates that building more roads merely induces additional traffic demand, inevitably resulting in recurrent gridlock within a few years.\n\nFurthermore, expanding public transit infrastructure promotes socio-economic equity. Modern roadways predominantly benefit private vehicle owners, whereas reliable public transport is a vital lifeline for lower-income demographics, students, and senior citizens, granting them equitable access to employment, education, and healthcare facilities. Subsidized, high-speed mass transit shrinks commuter travel times and lowers the cost of living for working families, stimulating widespread economic productivity.\n\nIn conclusion, while maintaining existing road safety is undeniably necessary, directing the majority of municipal infrastructure capital into sustainable, modern public transit networks produces far greater environmental preservation and social mobility. Governments should therefore prioritize mass transit over road expansion.",
